/**********************************************************
* Project 1: Metro Simulator
* CS 15
* README
*
*********************************************************/

Compile/run:
     - Compile using
            make MetroSim
     - run executable with
            ./MetroSim stationsFile passFile [commands]

Purpose
     The Metro Simulator is designed to simulate the operation of a metro 
     transit system, managing passengers and their movement between stations 
     using a queue-based system. The program reads input files defining station 
     information and passenger data and processes a series of commands to 
     simulate metro operations.

Acknowledgements
     Special thanks to the TAs who provided clarifications on file I/O 
     operations during lab sessions.

Files:

main.cpp:
     The main client file for the simulation, handles file inputs and making
     sure that calls to program are properly formatted

MetroSim.cpp:
     Implements the MetroSim class, representing the state of the simulations
     and altering it based on commands from cin or a command file

Passenger.cpp:
     Implements the Passenger class, representing an individual passenger in 
     the simulation.

Passenger.h:
     Defines the Passenger class and its member functions.

PassengerQueue.cpp:
     Implements the PassengerQueue class, which manages passengers using a 
     queue structure.

PassengerQueue.h:
     Defines the PassengerQueue class and its member functions.

unit_test.h:
     Contains unit tests for various program functionalities, ensuring 
     correctness.

output_test1.txt, output_test2.txt, output_test3.txt
     Output files written to by unit_test.h that are used for diff testing.

output_correct1.txt, output_correct2.txt, output_correct3.txt
     Pre-written files with the proper data to be used for diff testing.

Makefile:
     Automates the compilation process for the program.

test_cout.txt, test_cout2.txt, cout2a.txt:
     Output files that take in diverted cout data after running MetroSim file 
     to be used for diff testing.

cout_correct.txt, cout_correct2.txt, cout2b.txt:
     Output files that take in diverted cout data after running the provided 
     file theMetroSim to be used as the correct values for diff testing.

test_commands.txt, test_commands2.txt, test_short_commands, commands2.txt:
     Command files used for testing, read by MetroSim to discern functionality 
     for different cases the simulation could face.

unit_tests.h:
     A series of tests for various elements of the program to assert their
     general functionality or lack thereof.


Compile/run:
     - Compile using
            make MetroSim
     - run executable with
            ./MetroSim stationsFile passFile [commands]

Architectural Overview:

    The Metro Simulator consists of three main classes: Passenger, which 
    represents an individual passenger with attributes such as ID, starting
    station, and destination station. It also includes PassengerQueue, which
    implements a queue-based structure for managing passenger movement. The 
    program processes input files to initialize passenger and station data, 
    then follows the provided commands to simulate metro system operations.
    The last of the classes is MetroSim itself which holds two instances of a 
    struct named QueueData used to represent the train and the train stations 
    respectively and holds a name variable (to represent train station names, 
    the specific train cars did not receive names, though both have id 
    associated id numbers), and a PassengerQueue named queue. It also holds 
    an idCount variable that increments to be able to discern between the 
    various passengers and a currStation variable that tracks where the train 
    is currently located. The various functions within the MetroSim class 
    runs the majority of the processing, though there is also a client file 
    called main that handles some of the file input tasks. MetroSim is 
    included at the top of the main client file. 

Data Structures and Algorithms

     The primary data structure used in this program is std::list from the C++ 
     Standard Library. A doubly linked list is used to implement the 
     PassengerQueue, allowing for efficient insertions and deletions at both 
     ends. The most complex algorithm currently implemented is basis of the
     toString() function of PassengerQueue which takes in the whole of the 
     queue and prints out the data for each passenger one by one using the
     getters from the Passenger class.

Testing
     The program has been tested using unit tests implemented in unit_test.h. 
     the testing covers constructor validation, getter functions correctness 
     and print function output verification for the Passenger class as well as
     enqueue functionality ensuring proper order maintenance, dequeue 
     functionality ensuring correct element removal, and toString function 
     verifying the queue’s contents representation for the PassengerQueue class
     All tests were executed, and assertions were used to validate expected 
     outcomes. These tests ensure the correctness of the queue operations 
     and the accuracy of the metro simulation process. Additionally the print
     statements for Passenger and PassengerQueue were tested using a 
     combination of unit_test and using diff on correct versions of the files
     Additionally, MetroSim class functions, with the help of printState's cout
     information was redirected into output files that were tested against 
     those from theMetroSim and mindTheGap's and run's outputs (the combination
     of which make up the general outputs of the program) were directly diff 
     tested against the output file from various command files being used with
     theMetroSim. This ensured that print statements for passengers, stations, 
     and train movements matched the correct simulation behavior.


Part that you found most difficult:
     The specific details of the Passenger and PassengerQueue classes were not
     too bad to implement, though the fact that this project starts with much
     less constrictions added some unique challenges that I had to work through
     to figure out what I should work on first to get the program working and
     how all of the separate classes and data structures need to work together
     to make it all run properly. 

Time Spent: 19 hours
